   "etapa": "explicar",
   "piezas": [
    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Two ways of saying «you»",
     "texto": [
      "Spanish has two ways of addressing a single person:\n\n- **tú**: with friends, family and people you know well\n- **usted**: with strangers, older people or in formal situations\n\nThe important thing: **usted takes the verb in the third person**.\n\n- ¿Cómo estás **tú**? → ¿Cómo está **usted**?\n- ¿Tienes un momento? → ¿Tiene un momento?\n\nIn some regions **vos** is used instead of «tú». It isn't a mistake: it's another variety."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "gramatica",
     "titulo": "The verb changes with usted",
     "texto": [
      "When you use **usted**, the verb goes as it does with «él» or «ella»:\n\n- tú **estás** → usted **está**\n- tú **tienes** → usted **tiene**\n- tú **eres** → usted **es**\n\nAnd the pronoun **usted** is written out, because it's what marks the form of address. «¿Cómo está?» could be él, ella or usted; «¿Cómo está usted?» leaves no doubt."
     ],
